              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "The best and most efficient way to change the color of a `<span>` on hover is by using **CSS**. Alternatively, you can do it using **JavaScript** if you need it to trigger other dynamic events. \n\nHere are both methods:\n\n---\n\n### Method 1: Using CSS (Recommended)\nThis is the cleanest approach. It uses the CSS `:hover` pseudo-class and keeps your HTML structure separate from your styling.\n\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<style>\n  /* 1. Set the default styling for the span */\n  .color-change {\n    color: black; \n    cursor: pointer; /* Changes the mouse cursor to a pointer (optional) */\n    transition: color 0.3s ease; /* Makes the color change smooth (optional) */\n  }\n\n  /* 2. Define the hover color */\n  .color-change:hover {\n    color: #ff4757; /* The color it changes to when hovered */\n  }\n</style>\n</head>\n<body>\n\n  <p>Hover over this <span class=\"color-change\">text right here</span> to watch it change color!</p>\n\n</body>\n</html>\n```\n\n---\n\n### Method 2: Using Inline JavaScript (Quickest)\nIf you cannot edit your external stylesheet and need a quick inline solution, you can use the `onmouseover` and `onmouseout` attributes directly on the HTML tag.\n\n```html\n<p>Hover over this \n  <span \n    style=\"color: black;\" \n    onmouseover=\"this.style.color='red'\" \n    onmouseout=\"this.style.color='black'\">\n    text right here\n  </span> \nto watch it change color!\n</p>\n```\n\n---\n\n### Method 3: Using Modern JavaScript (For dynamic applications)\nIf you are building an application where the color needs to change dynamically or you prefer to keep your JavaScript separated from your HTML:\n\n```html\n<p>Hover over this <span id=\"my-span\">text right here</span>!</p>\n\n<script>\n  const span = document.getElementById('my-span');\n\n  span.addEventListener('mouseenter', () => {\n    span.style.color = 'blue';\n  });\n\n  span.addEventListener('mouseleave', () => {\n    span.style.color = 'black'; // Resets the color when mouse leaves\n  });\n</script>\n```\n\n### Which should you use?\n* **Use Method 1 (CSS)** for 95% of use cases. It is lightweight, performs better, and handles touch devices and accessibility more gracefully.\n* **Use Method 2 or 3 (JavaScript)** only if the color needs to change dynamically based on logic, API calls, or database settings.",
